AWS Learner Lab & Instance Launch (prerequisite to everything)
Log into AWS Academy Learner Lab → click Start Lab

EC2 → Launch Instance:
Name: DevOps-Final-Exam-2026B
AMI: Amazon Linux 2023 (default)
Instance type: t3.micro
Key pair: Create new key pair → name it e.g. devops_final_key → type RSA → format .pem → Create (this downloads to your Windows Downloads folder)
Security group: create new, name it e.g. DevOps_Final_SG, allow inbound:
SSH (22) — Anywhere
Custom TCP 8080 — Anywhere (Jenkins/Tomcat default)
Custom TCP 8086 — Anywhere (Docker container)
Custom TCP 9000-9999 — Anywhere (covers Tomcat 9xxx port + Minikube NodePort range flexibility)
Storage: 15 GB (Jenkins needs headroom — bump from the 8 GB default)
Launch instance.
Wait until it shows Running, then copy its Public IPv4 address.

Lock down your key on Windows (Command Prompt, run as Administrator):

cd %USERPROFILE%\Homework
icacls devops_final_key.pem /reset
icacls devops_final_key.pem /inheritance:r
icacls devops_final_key.pem /grant:r "%USERNAME%:(R)"

Connect:
ssh -i "devops_final_key.pem" ec2-user@<EC2-Public-IP>

EC2 Setup, SSH Authentication & Repository Setup

Since your instance is fresh, cleanup is trivially satisfied — but screenshot the empty EC2/AMI/Snapshots pages anyway for the deliverable.

Set the hostname:

sudo su -
nano /etc/hostname

Replace contents with (your actual student ID):

s123456-final-exam-2026b

Save (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter):

reboot

Wait ~60 seconds, reconnect:

ssh -i "devops_final_key.pem" ec2-user@<EC2-Public-IP>
sudo su -

Confirm your prompt now reads root@s123456-final-exam-2026b:~#.

Install Git first (needed before anything repo-related):

yum install git -y
git --version

Generate SSH key and connect to GitHub:

ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -C "your_email@example.com"

Press Enter through all prompts (default location, empty passphrase).

cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

Copy the output → GitHub.com → Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH key → paste → Add.

ssh -T git@github.com

Type yes if prompted; confirm the "Hi username! You've successfully authenticated" message.

Configure Git identity:

git config --global user.name "Your Full Name"
git config --global user.email "your_email@example.com"

Clone your assigned repo:
cd ~
git clone git@github.com:rmit-vietnam-computing-technologies/<your-assigned-repo>.git
cd <your-assigned-repo>

Edit README.md on main:

nano README.md

Replace placeholders with course code. Save, then:
git add README.md
git commit -m "Edit the README file"
git push

Create develop branch + question-1 folder:

git checkout -b develop
mkdir question-1
cp README.md question-1/
git add question-1
git commit -m "Set up develop branch and question-1 folder"
git push -u origin develop
